      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Specific resistance loops appear in different shapes influenced by different resistive properties of the airways, yet their descriptive ability is compressed to a single parameter - its slope. We aimed to develop new parameters reflecting the various shapes of the loop and to explore their potential in the characterisation of obstructive airways diseases."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Our study included 134 subjects: Healthy controls (N\u2009=\u200922), Asthma with non-obstructive lung function (N\u2009=\u200922) and COPD of all disease stages (N\u2009=\u200990). Different shapes were described by geometrical and second-order transfer function parameters."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Our parameters demonstrated no difference between asthma and healthy controls groups, but were significantly different (p\u2009<\u20090.0001) from the patients with COPD. Grouping mild COPD subjects by an open or not-open shape of the resistance loop revealed significant differences of loop parameters and classical lung function parameters. Multiple logistic regression indicated RV/TLC as the only predictor of loop opening with OR\u2009=\u20091.157, 95% CI (1.064-1.267), p-value\u2009=\u20090.0006 and R(2)\u2009=\u20090.35. Inducing airway narrowing in asthma gave equal shape measures as in COPD non-openers, but with a decreased slope (p\u2009<\u20090.0001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This study introduces new parameters calculated from the resistance loops which may correlate with different phenotypes of obstructive airways diseases."
        }
      ]},
